\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Example for determining the mixture density in face centered control volumes. In this case, the face is $(i+1/2,j)$. Momentum conservation is guaranteed since the density for each material is discretized on the refined grid $(2i+1,2j)$, $(2i+1,2j+1)$, $(2i+2,2j)$, and $(2i+2,2j+1)$. The Fluid regions tessellate the computational domain ($\Omega _{1}$, $\Omega _{1,Ghost}$, $\Omega _{2}$, $\Omega _{2,Ghost}$) and the elastic material regions ($\Omega _{3}$) are embedded within the computational domain. Prior to computing mixture density, we use the nested dissection algorithm in order to convert the material reconstruction so that Fluid and Elastic materials tessellate the computational domain ($\Omega _{1}$, $\Omega _{2}$, $\Omega _{3}$). }}{22}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{MAC_density_fig}{{5}{22}{Example for determining the mixture density in face centered control volumes. In this case, the face is $(i+1/2,j)$. Momentum conservation is guaranteed since the density for each material is discretized on the refined grid $(2i+1,2j)$, $(2i+1,2j+1)$, $(2i+2,2j)$, and $(2i+2,2j+1)$. The Fluid regions tessellate the computational domain ($\Omega _{1}$, $\Omega _{1,Ghost}$, $\Omega _{2}$, $\Omega _{2,Ghost}$) and the elastic material regions ($\Omega _{3}$) are embedded within the computational domain. Prior to computing mixture density, we use the nested dissection algorithm in order to convert the material reconstruction so that Fluid and Elastic materials tessellate the computational domain ($\Omega _{1}$, $\Omega _{2}$, $\Omega _{3}$)}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Illustration for associating material parcels at $t=t^{n}$ to material parcels at the new time $t=t^{n+1}$. In panels (a) and (c), the material parcels intersect so that the ``weight of association'' is given by the intersected mass. In panel (b), the parcel at $t^{n}$ does not intersection the nearby parcel at $t^{n+1}$, but since they are within two grid cells from one another, they have a weight $\epsilon $ which is considered to be arbitrarily close to zero. In panel (d), parcel 2 at time $t^{n+1}$ came from the coagulation of flotsam at time $t^{n}$. This parcel is an ``orphan'' parcel and is associated with weight $\epsilon $ with the parcel with the nearest centroid. }}{23}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{mass_cor_fig}{{6}{23}{Illustration for associating material parcels at $t=t^{n}$ to material parcels at the new time $t=t^{n+1}$. In panels (a) and (c), the material parcels intersect so that the ``weight of association'' is given by the intersected mass. In panel (b), the parcel at $t^{n}$ does not intersection the nearby parcel at $t^{n+1}$, but since they are within two grid cells from one another, they have a weight $\epsilon $ which is considered to be arbitrarily close to zero. In panel (d), parcel 2 at time $t^{n+1}$ came from the coagulation of flotsam at time $t^{n}$. This parcel is an ``orphan'' parcel and is associated with weight $\epsilon $ with the parcel with the nearest centroid}{figure.6}{}}
